  "strategy": "1. **Approach and Rationale**  \n   We address the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by optimizing a step function \\(h: [0,2] \\to [0,1]\\) with \\(\\int h(x)  dx = 1\\) to minimize the maximum overlap \\(C_5 = \\max_k \\int h(x)(1-h(x+k))  dx\\). The key challenge is the non-convex nature of the objective function and the discontinuity of the correlation-based evaluation. Our approach is a multi-resolution optimization scheme using spectral representations (Chebyshev and Fej\u00e9r kernels) to explore the solution space efficiently. We reduce dimensionality by approximating step functions with continuous bases, enabling gradient-based optimization. The method relies on transferring solutions between resolutions via interpolation and amplitude scaling for consistent integrals.  \n\n2. **Mathematical Specification**  \n   - **Variables**:  \n     - \\(n_{\\text{points}}\\) points discretizing \\([0,2]\\).  \n     - Step function \\(h\\) approximated by a continuous basis \\( \\mathbb{R}^{n_{\\text{points}}} \\).  \n   - **Objective**:  \n     - Minimize \\(C_5 = \\max_k (\\text{np.correlate}(h, 1-h, \\text{mode}='full') \\times dx\\), with \\(dx = 2/n_{\\text{points}}\\).  \n   - **Constraints**:  \n     - \\(0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1\\) for all \\(i\\).  \n     - \\(\\sum h = n_{\\text{points}}/2\\).  \n   - **Algorithm Selection**:  \n     - **Kernel-based**: Use Chebyshev and Fej\u00e9r kernel bases for smoothness.  \n     - **Optimization**: `scipy.optimize.minimize` with SLSQP or trust-constr to handle bounds and equality constraints.  \n     - **Repair and Projection**: For invalid solutions, project onto the integral/box constraints using linear interpolation and resampling.  \n\n3. **Implementation Procedure**  \n   **Initialization**:  \n   - Validate inputs: Check \\(0 \\leq \\text{initial\\_h\\_values}[i] \\leq 1\\) and \\(\\sum \\text{initial\\_h\\_values} \\approx n_{\\text{points}}/2\\).  \n   - Set up parameter files for each resolution \\(n_{\\text{steps}}\\) (parent, doubled resolutions).  \n\n   **Multi-resolution Loop**:  \n   - **Level Setup** (`init_level(n)`)\uff1a  \n     - Interpolate parent solution to \\(n\\) points.  \n     - Adjust amplitude so \\(\\sum h = n/2\\).  \n     - Define bounds and objective function.  \n   - **Kernel Projection** (`project_kernel(params, n)`)\uff1a  \n     - Project solution onto kernel spaces (Chebyshev, Fej\u00e9r) to enforce smoothness.  \n   - **Optimization**:  \n     - Use `minimize` with trust-constr for constrained problems.  \n     - Constraints: \\((-1 \\leq x_i \\leq 1)\\) and \\(\\sum x_i = 0\\).  \n   - **Evaluation**: Compute \\(C_5\\) via correlation.  \n\n   **Resampling and Repair**:  \n   - If a resolution step misses the feasible set, resample from bounds ensuring \\(\\sum h = n/2\\).  \n   - **IntegrateKernel()**: Weight samples by kernel coefficients for smoother initial guesses.  \n\n   **Termination Check**: Accept best solution if valid.  \n\n4. **Search Choices and Variations**  \n   - **Kernel Variations**: Chebyshev kernel (T\u2096), Fej\u00e9r kernel (F\u2096).  \n   - **Resolution Plan**: Start at \\(n= \\text{parent}\\), then \\(n=2\\times\\text{parent}\\), \\(n=4\\times\\text{parent}\\), \\(n=8\\times\\text{parent}\\).  \n   - **Initializations**: 4 per resolution (Chebyshev init, Fej\u00e9r init, random init, linear parent interpolation).  \n\n   **Adaptive Checks**:  \n   - Exit early if a run exceeds integral/box constraints persistently.  \n\n5. **Compute Budget and Robustness**  \n   - **Time Estimation**: Each \\(n\\)-point optimization costs ~\\(O(n^2)\\) operations.  \n   - **Fallback**: Use lower-resolution initial trajectories.  \n   - **Budget Allocation**: Distribute time across resolutions. Each optimization runs until target \\(n\\) is reached.  \n\n6. **Validation and Return Contract**  \n   - Check \\(0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1\\), \\(\\sum h = n_{\\text{points}}/2\\), \\(C_5\\)-score valid.  \n   - Validate objective via recomputation.  \n   - Return best solution found.",
